[[:db/cas 17592186088372 :question/worked-solution nil "The characteristic equation is\n$$\n\\lambda^2+1=0,\n$$\nso $\\lambda=\\pm\\mathrm{i}.$ The general real-valued solution is\n$$\ny=P\\cos x+Q\\sin x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186086885 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186086886] [:db/cas 17592186087596 :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y = e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda e^{\\lambda t}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+85{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +85) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 85 = 0,\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +85 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+85 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+81 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-81 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 9\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 9\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\lambda = 2 \\pm 9 \\textrm{i}$ are the complex roots of the characteristic equation, the general solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny = e^{2t} \\left( P \\cos 9t + Q \\sin 9t \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $P=-2$ and $Q=7.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II does *not* take the above form. It contains $\\sin 3t,$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III takes the above form with $P=0$ and $Q=-6.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "Assuming $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda e^{\\lambda t}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+85{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +85) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 85 = 0,\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +85 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+85 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+81 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-81 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 9\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 9\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\lambda = 2 \\pm 9 \\textrm{i}$ are the complex roots of the characteristic equation, the general solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny = e^{2t} \\left( P \\cos 9t + Q \\sin 9t \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $P=-2$ and $Q=7.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II does *not* take the above form. It contains $\\sin 3t,$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III takes the above form with $P=0$ and $Q=-6.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186088236 :question/worked-solution nil "The characteristic equation is\n$$\n\\lambda^2-2\\lambda+17=(\\lambda-1)^2+16=0,\n$$\nso the roots are $1\\pm4\\mathrm{i}.$ Hence every solution has the form\n$$\ny=e^x(P\\cos4x+Q\\sin4x).\n$$\nThe only listed function outside this form is $5e^{2x}\\sin4x.$ Indeed,\n$$\ny''-2y'+17y=5e^{2x}(\\sin4x+8\\cos4x)\\ne0.\n$$\nTherefore, $5e^{2x}\\sin4x$ is the only non-solution."] [:db/cas 17592186086070 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186086074] [:db/cas 17592186088237 :question/worked-solution nil "The characteristic equation is\n$$\n\\lambda^2-10\\lambda+29=(\\lambda-5)^2+4=0,\n$$\nso the roots are $5\\pm2\\mathrm{i}.$ Thus every solution has the form\n$$\ny=e^{5t}(P\\cos2t+Q\\sin2t).\n$$\nThe only listed function outside this form is $5\\sin2t.$ Substitution gives\n$$\ny''-10y'+29y=125\\sin2t-100\\cos2t\\ne0.\n$$\nTherefore, $5\\sin2t$ is the only non-solution."] [:db/cas 17592186086076 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186086080] [:db/cas 17592186088352 :question/worked-solution nil "The characteristic equation is\n$$\n\\lambda^2-4\\lambda+13=(\\lambda-2)^2+9=0.\n$$\nIts roots are $2\\pm3\\mathrm{i},$ so the general solution is\n$$\ny=e^{2x}(P\\cos3x+Q\\sin3x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186086765 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186086769] [:db/cas 17592186078339 :question/worked-solution "This is a second-order homogeneous ODE. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=e^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = -6$ and $\\lambda = - 1.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6x} + Be^{- x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=-3$ and $B=0.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=2$ and $B=-1.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^x,$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "This is a second-order homogeneous ODE. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=e^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = -6$ and $\\lambda = - 1.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6x} + Be^{- x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=-3$ and $B=0.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=2$ and $B=-4.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^x,$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186151994 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + y - 2y =0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y - 2y =0,\n\\ \\ y(1) = e, \\ \\ y'(1) = e ^{-2}.\n$$" "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + y' - 2y =0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y' - 2y =0,\n\\ \\ y(1) = e, \\ \\ y'(1) = e ^{-2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186151994 :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=e$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e & =A{e}^{1}+B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ e & =Ae+B{e}^{-2} \\\\ A & =1-B{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = e^{-2},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = Ae^x - 2Be^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1)=e^{-2}$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =A{e}^{1}-2B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ {e}^{-2} & =Ae-2B{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=1-B{e}^{-3} \\\\ {e}^{-2} &=Ae-2B{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A = 1 - B e ^{-3}$ in the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =(1-B{e}^{-3})e-2B{e}^{-2} \\\\ 1 & ={e}^{3}-B-2B \\\\ B & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $B= \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3$ back into the equation $A = 1-Be^{-3}$ gives\n\n$$\nA = 1 -  \\dfrac {(e ^ 3 - 1) e ^ {-3}} 3 =   \\dfrac {2 +  e ^ {-3}} 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac {  e ^ {-3} + 2} 3  e^{x} + \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3 e^{-2x}.\n$$" "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=e$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e & =A{e}^{1}+B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ e & =Ae+B{e}^{-2} \\\\ A & =1-B{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = e^{-2},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = Ae^x - 2Be^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1)=e^{-2}$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =A{e}^{1}-2B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ {e}^{-2} & =Ae-2B{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=1-B{e}^{-3} \\\\ {e}^{-2} &=Ae-2B{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A = 1 - B e ^{-3}$ in the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =(1-B{e}^{-3})e-2B{e}^{-2} \\\\ 1 & ={e}^{3}-B-2B \\\\ B & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $B= \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3$ back into the equation $A = 1-Be^{-3}$ gives\n\n$$\nA = 1 -  \\dfrac {(e ^ 3 - 1) e ^ {-3}} 3 =   \\dfrac {2 +  e ^ {-3}} 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac {  e ^ {-3} + 2} 3  e^{x} + \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3 e^{-2x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345349 :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-14{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -14) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -7)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =7.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA{e}^{-2}+B{e}^{7}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}+7B{e}^{7x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-2A{e}^{-2}+7B{e}^{7}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-2}+B{e}^{7} &=2 \\\\ -2A{e}^{-2}+7B{e}^{7} &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A={e}^{2},B={e}^{-7}.$\n\nNow we get the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-2x}+{e}^{-7}\\cdot {e}^{7x} \\\\ y & ={e}^{-2x+2}+{e}^{7x-7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-14{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -14) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -7)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =7.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA{e}^{-2}+B{e}^{7}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}+7B{e}^{7x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-2A{e}^{-2}+7B{e}^{7}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-2}+B{e}^{7} &=2 \\\\ -2A{e}^{-2}+7B{e}^{7} &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A={e}^{2},B={e}^{-7}.$\n\nNow we get the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-2x}+{e}^{-7}\\cdot {e}^{7x} \\\\ y & ={e}^{-2x+2}+{e}^{7x-7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186152779 :question/worked-solution nil "The characteristic equation is\n$$\n\\lambda^2+10\\lambda+25=(\\lambda+5)^2=0.\n$$\nThe repeated root $-5$ gives $y=Ae^{-5x}+Bxe^{-5x}.$ From $y(0)=5,$ we obtain $A=5.$ Differentiating gives\n$$\ny'=-5Ae^{-5x}+Be^{-5x}-5Bxe^{-5x}.\n$$\nUsing $y'(0)=17$ yields $-5A+B=17,$ so $B=42.$ Therefore,\n$$\ny=5e^{-5x}+42xe^{-5x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186151181 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186151185] [:db/cas 17592186348067 :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{9}{4}{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}\\left({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4}\\right) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\left(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2}\\right) & =0 \\\\ {\\left(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x/2}+Bx{e}^{-3x/2}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \nSubstituting $y(1)={e}^{-1/2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3/2}+B{e}^{-3/2} & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\ A+B & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3/2x}+B{e}^{-3/2x}-\\frac{3}{2}Bx{e}^{-3/2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}B{e}^{-3/2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}B & =\\frac{1}{2}e \\\\ -3A-B & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B &=e \\\\ -3A-B &=e\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-e,B=2e.$\n\nNow we get the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-e\\cdot {e}^{-3x/2}+2e\\cdot x{e}^{-3x/2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x/2+1}+2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{9}{4}{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}\\left({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4}\\right) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\left(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2}\\right) & =0 \\\\ {\\left(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{2}$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x/2}+Bx{e}^{-3x/2}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \nSubstituting $y(1)={e}^{-1/2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3/2}+B{e}^{-3/2} & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\ A+B & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3/2x}+B{e}^{-3/2x}-\\frac{3}{2}Bx{e}^{-3/2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}B{e}^{-3/2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}B & =\\frac{1}{2}e \\\\ -3A-B & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B &=e \\\\ -3A-B &=e\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-e,B=2e.$\n\nNow we get the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-e\\cdot {e}^{-3x/2}+2e\\cdot x{e}^{-3x/2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x/2+1}+2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393765 :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA{e}^{4}+B{e}^{4}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{4x}+4Bx{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4A{e}^{4}+5B{e}^{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4}+B{e}^{4} &=2 \\\\ 4A{e}^{4}+5B{e}^{4} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=11{e}^{-4},B=-9{e}^{-4}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11{e}^{-4}\\cdot {e}^{4x}-9{e}^{-4}\\cdot x{e}^{4x} \\\\  & =11{e}^{4x-4}-9x{e}^{4x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA{e}^{4}+B{e}^{4}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{4x}+4Bx{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=-1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4A{e}^{4}+5B{e}^{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4}+B{e}^{4} &=2 \\\\ 4A{e}^{4}+5B{e}^{4} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=11{e}^{-4},B=-9{e}^{-4}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11{e}^{-4}\\cdot {e}^{4x}-9{e}^{-4}\\cdot x{e}^{4x} \\\\  & =11{e}^{4x-4}-9x{e}^{4x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186152403 :question/worked-solution nil "The characteristic roots are $\\lambda=\\pm\\sqrt{11}\\mathrm{i},$ giving\n$$\ny=P\\cos(\\sqrt{11}x)+Q\\sin(\\sqrt{11}x).\n$$\nThe first condition gives $P=7.$ Differentiating,\n$$\ny'=-\\sqrt{11}P\\sin(\\sqrt{11}x)+\\sqrt{11}Q\\cos(\\sqrt{11}x).\n$$\nThus $y'(0)=-4\\sqrt{11}$ gives $\\sqrt{11}Q=-4\\sqrt{11},$ so $Q=-4.$ Therefore,\n$$\ny=7\\cos(\\sqrt{11}x)-4\\sin(\\sqrt{11}x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186148949 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186148951] [:db/cas 17592186152008 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 6y' + 25y=0, \\quad y(0)= 2,  \\quad y'(0) = 5, \\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 6y' + 25y=0, \\quad y(0)= 2,  \\quad y'(0) = 5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186152008 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( P \\cos 4x + Q \\sin 4x \\right)\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2 = P.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 5,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{e}^{3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+{e}^{3x}(-4P\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0) = 5$ and $P=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =3{e}^{0}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)+{e}^{0}(-4(2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\ 5 & =6+4Q \\\\ -1 & =4Q \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( 2 \\cos 4x - \\dfrac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\right).\n$$" "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( P \\cos 4x + Q \\sin 4x \\right)\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2 = P.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 5,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{e}^{3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+{e}^{3x}(-4P\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0) = 5$ and $P=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =3{e}^{0}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)+{e}^{0}(-4(2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\ 5 & =6+4Q \\\\ -1 & =4Q \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( 2 \\cos 4x - \\dfrac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186152404 :question/worked-solution nil "The characteristic equation is\n$$\n\\lambda^2+2\\lambda+5=(\\lambda+1)^2+4=0,\n$$\nwith roots $-1\\pm2\\mathrm{i}.$ Therefore,\n$$\ny=e^{-x}(P\\cos2x+Q\\sin2x).\n$$\nThe condition $y(0)=3$ gives $P=3.$ Differentiating gives\n$$\ny'=-e^{-x}(P\\cos2x+Q\\sin2x)+e^{-x}(-2P\\sin2x+2Q\\cos2x).\n$$\nUsing $y'(0)=-5$ gives $-P+2Q=-5,$ hence $Q=-1.$ Therefore,\n$$\ny=e^{-x}(3\\cos2x-\\sin2x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186148955 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186148956] [:db/cas 17592186152799 :question/worked-solution nil "The characteristic roots are $3\\pm5\\mathrm{i},$ so\n$$\ny=e^{3x}(P\\cos5x+Q\\sin5x).\n$$\nThe first condition gives $P=5.$ Differentiating,\n$$\ny'=3e^{3x}(P\\cos5x+Q\\sin5x)+e^{3x}(-5P\\sin5x+5Q\\cos5x).\n$$\nUsing $y'(0)=10$ gives $3P+5Q=10,$ hence $Q=-1.$ Therefore,\n$$\ny=e^{3x}(5\\cos5x-\\sin5x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186151301 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186151302] [:db/cas 17592186431532 :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+10{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)^{2}-1+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-\\pi /6} & =1 \\\\ Q & ={e}^{\\pi /6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+{e}^{-x}(-3P\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-Q{e}^{-\\pi /6}-3P{e}^{-\\pi /6} & =2 \\\\ -Q-3P & =2{e}^{\\pi /6} \\\\ -{e}^{\\pi /6}-3P & =2{e}^{\\pi /6} \\\\ P & =-{e}^{\\pi /6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $Q={e}^{\\pi /6}$ and $P=-{e}^{\\pi /6}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-x}(-{e}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+{e}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x+\\pi /6}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+10{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-\\pi /6} & =1 \\\\ Q & ={e}^{\\pi /6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+{e}^{-x}(-3P\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-Q{e}^{-\\pi /6}-3P{e}^{-\\pi /6} & =2 \\\\ -Q-3P & =2{e}^{\\pi /6} \\\\ -{e}^{\\pi /6}-3P & =2{e}^{\\pi /6} \\\\ P & =-{e}^{\\pi /6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $Q={e}^{\\pi /6}$ and $P=-{e}^{\\pi /6}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-x}(-{e}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+{e}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x+\\pi /6}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186431535 :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1)-1+2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda -1 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =1\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=-2.\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ We differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{e}^{x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-2+Q=-4\\,⟹\\,Q=-2.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-2$ and $Q=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$" "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1)-1+2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda -1 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =1\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=-2.\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-4.$ We differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{e}^{x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-2+Q=-4\\,⟹\\,Q=-2.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-2$ and $Q=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186148190 :question/worked-solution nil "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-15t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+27{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 3\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-15\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+27{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[3\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-15\\lambda +27] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-15\\lambda +27 & =0 \\\\ 3{\\lambda }^{2}-18\\lambda +27 & =0 \\\\ 3({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9) & =0 \\\\ 3(\\lambda -3)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =3.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{3}+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln} t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186146894 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186146898] [:db/cas 17592186148186 :question/worked-solution nil "The characteristic equation is\n$$\n\\lambda(\\lambda-1)+7\\lambda+9=\\lambda^2+6\\lambda+9=(\\lambda+3)^2=0.\n$$\nThe repeated root $-3$ gives\n$$\ny=c_1x^{-3}+c_2x^{-3}\\ln x.\n$$\nThe condition $y(1)=3$ gives $c_1=3.$ Differentiating,\n$$\ny'=-3c_1x^{-4}+c_2x^{-4}-3c_2x^{-4}\\ln x.\n$$\nUsing $y'(1)=-8$ gives $-3c_1+c_2=-8,$ so $c_2=1.$ Therefore, for $x>0,$\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{3+\\ln x}{x^3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186146870 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186146875] [:db/cas 17592186148191 :question/worked-solution nil "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-6t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+8{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-6\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+8{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-6\\lambda +8] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-6\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ 2({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4) & =0 \\\\ 2(\\lambda -2)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =2.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln} t.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=3.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2t{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}t+2{c}_{2}t\\operatorname{ln} t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=3$ and ${c}_{1}=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 3 & =0+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln} t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186146900 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186146903] [:db/cas 17592186357596 :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=1.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ and ${c}_{1}=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =1+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x+3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$" "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=4.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=4$ and ${c}_{1}=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =1+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x+3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357599 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-17t{y}^{′}+81y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{t}^{9}+{c}_{2}{t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-17t{y}^{′}+81y,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=3,\\,t>0.\n$$" "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-17t{y}^{′}+81y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{t}^{9}+{c}_{2}{t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-17t{y}^{′}+81y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=3,\\,t>0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186649848 :question/worked-solution "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+9t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+16{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+9\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+16{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+9\\lambda +16] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+9\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =-4.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{-4}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}+\\frac{{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{{t}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n3={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=3.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-\\frac{4{c}_{1}}{{t}^{5}}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{t}^{5}}-\\frac{4{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)}{{t}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=3$ and ${c}_{1}=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 3 & =-12+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{3+15\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{{t}^{8}}.\n$$" "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+9t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+16{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+9\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+16{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+9\\lambda +16] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+9\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =-4.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{-4}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}+\\frac{{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{{t}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n3={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=3.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-\\frac{4{c}_{1}}{{t}^{5}}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{t}^{5}}-\\frac{4{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)}{{t}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=3$ and ${c}_{1}=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 3 & =-12+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{3+15\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{{t}^{4}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088232 :question/worked-solution nil "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ gives the characteristic equation\n$$\n\\lambda^2+2\\lambda+1=(\\lambda+1)^2=0.\n$$\nThus $\\lambda=-1$ is a repeated root."] [:db/cas 17592186086046 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186086047] [:db/cas 17592186088238 :question/worked-solution nil "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ gives\n$$\n\\lambda^2-16\\lambda+64=(\\lambda-8)^2=0.\n$$\nThus $\\lambda=8$ is a repeated root."] [:db/cas 17592186086082 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186086086] [:db/cas 17592186088346 :question/worked-solution nil "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ gives the characteristic equation\n$$\n\\lambda^2+6\\lambda+9=(\\lambda+3)^2=0.\n$$\nThe repeated root is $\\lambda=-3,$ so the general solution is\n$$\ny=Ae^{-3x}+Bxe^{-3x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186086729 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186086730] [:db/cas 17592186088233 :question/worked-solution nil "The characteristic equation is\n$$\n\\lambda^2-4\\lambda+4=(\\lambda-2)^2=0.\n$$\nThe repeated root $\\lambda=2$ gives the general solution\n$$\ny=Ae^{2x}+Bxe^{2x}.\n$$\nAll the given functions have this form except $2e^x.$ Substitution of $y=2e^x$ gives\n$$\ny''-4y'+4y=2e^x-8e^x+8e^x=2e^x\\ne0.\n$$\nTherefore, $2e^x$ is the only non-solution."] [:db/cas 17592186086052 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186086056] [:db/cas 17592186148187 :question/worked-solution nil "A second-order Cauchy-Euler equation has the form\n$$\nax^2y''+bxy'+cy=f(x),\n$$\nwhere $a,b,c$ are constants and $a\\ne0.$ The listed equation\n$$\n3x^2y''-2xy'+2y=2\\ln x\n$$\nhas $a=3,$ $b=-2,$ $c=2,$ and $f(x)=2\\ln x.$ Each other option has a derivative or $y$ coefficient with the wrong power of $x.$ Therefore, this is the only listed Cauchy-Euler equation."] [:db/cas 17592186146876 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186146878] [:db/cas 17592186148183 :question/worked-solution nil "For a Cauchy-Euler equation, use the trial solution $y=x^\\lambda.$ Then $y'=\\lambda x^{\\lambda-1}$ and $y''=\\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}.$ Substitution gives the characteristic equation\n$$\n\\lambda(\\lambda-1)-\\lambda-3=\\lambda^2-2\\lambda-3=(\\lambda-3)(\\lambda+1)=0.\n$$\nThe distinct roots are $3$ and $-1.$ Therefore, for $x>0,$\n$$\ny(x)=c_1x^3+c_2x^{-1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186146852 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186146855] [:db/cas 17592186148189 :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -4] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\pm 2.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-2}={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{c}_{1}x-2\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\ 2 & ={c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ 2 & ={c}_{1}-(-{c}_{1}) \\\\ {c}_{1} & =1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -4] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\pm 2.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-2}={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=4.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{c}_{1}x-2\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\ 2 & ={c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ 2 & ={c}_{1}-(-{c}_{1}) \\\\ {c}_{1} & =1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335759 :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-3t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+3{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-3\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+3{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +3] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =3.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(2)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0=2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-4{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(2)=8.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(2)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & ={c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}(2)^{2} \\\\ 8 & ={c}_{1}+12{c}_{2} \\\\ 8 & =-4{c}_{2}+12{c}_{2} \\\\ 8 & =8{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{3}-4t.\n$$" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-3t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+3{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-3\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+3{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +3] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =3.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(2)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0=2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-4{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(2)=8.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(2)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & ={c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}(2)^{2} \\\\ 8 & ={c}_{1}+12{c}_{2} \\\\ 8 & =-4{c}_{2}+12{c}_{2} \\\\ 8 & =8{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{3}-4t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186370979 :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+8x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-3{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+8\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-3{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+8\\lambda -3] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+8\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda -1)(2\\lambda +3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{2}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-3/2}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}{x}^{-1/2}}{2}-\\frac{3{c}_{2}{x}^{-5/2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{3(-{c}_{1})}{2} \\\\ 2 & =2{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)={x}^{1/2}-{x}^{-3/2}.\n$$" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+8x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-3{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+8\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-3{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+8\\lambda -3] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+8\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda -1)(2\\lambda +3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{2}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-3/2}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}{x}^{-1/2}}{2}-\\frac{3{c}_{2}{x}^{-5/2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{3(-{c}_{1})}{2} \\\\ 2 & =2{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)={x}^{1/2}-{x}^{-3/2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186645757 :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+3t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}-6{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+3\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }-6{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+3\\lambda -6] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+3\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(2\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{t}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=1-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-2{c}_{1}{t}^{-3}+\\frac{3}{2}{c}_{2}{t}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{3}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ 5 & =-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{3}{2}(1-{c}_{1}) \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & =-\\frac{7}{2}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =-1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=-{t}^{-2}+2{t}^{3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+2{t}^{3/2}.\n$$" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+3t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}-6{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+3\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }-6{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+3\\lambda -6] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+3\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(2\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=1-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-2{c}_{1}{t}^{-3}+\\frac{3}{2}{c}_{2}{t}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{3}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ 5 & =-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{3}{2}(1-{c}_{1}) \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & =-\\frac{7}{2}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =-1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=-{t}^{-2}+2{t}^{3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+2{t}^{3/2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186148754 :tutorial/content "When the characteristic equation of a Cauchy-Euler equation has a repeated root $\\lambda = c,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = c_1 x^c + c_2 x^c \\ln x.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's solve the following Cauchy-Euler equation:\n\n$$\nx^2y''-xy'+y=0\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the trial solution $y = x^\\lambda$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-x\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }(\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation has a double root $\\lambda = 1.$ Therefore, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={c}_{1}{x}^{1}+{c}_{2}{x}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For $x>0,$ when the characteristic equation of a Cauchy-Euler equation has a repeated root $\\lambda = c,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = c_1 x^c + c_2 x^c \\ln x.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's solve the following Cauchy-Euler equation:\n\n$$\nx^2y''-xy'+y=0\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the trial solution $y = x^\\lambda$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-x\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }(\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation has a double root $\\lambda = 1.$ Therefore, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={c}_{1}{x}^{1}+{c}_{2}{x}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186148748 :tutorial/content "The second-order Cauchy-Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\nax^2y''+bxy'+cy=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nCauchy-Euler equations can be solved using a trial solution $y=x^\\lambda,$ where $\\lambda$ is a constant to be found. To illustrate, let's solve the following Cauchy-Euler equation:\n\n$$\nx^2y''+xy'-9y=0\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the trial solution $y = x^\\lambda$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-9{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-9{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }(\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -9) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={c}_{1}{x}^{3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-3} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{x}^{3}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The second-order Cauchy-Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\nax^2y''+bxy'+cy=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants and $a\\ne0.$ We work on $x>0.$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation ($f(x)=0$) can be solved using a trial solution $y=x^\\lambda,$ where $\\lambda$ is a constant to be found. To illustrate, let's solve the following Cauchy-Euler equation:\n\n$$\nx^2y''+xy'-9y=0\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the trial solution $y = x^\\lambda$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-9{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-9{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }(\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -9) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={c}_{1}{x}^{3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-3} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{x}^{3}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/retract 17592186148184 :question/answer-fields 17592186146858] [:db/retract 17592186148189 :question/answer-fields 17592186146888] [:db/ensure 17592186148754 :tutorial/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186148748 :tutorial/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186078339 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186087596 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186088232 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186088233 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186088236 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186088237 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186088238 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186088346 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186088352 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186088372 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186148183 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186148184 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186148186 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186148187 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186148189 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186148190 :question/validate] 